Like, you know what I mean?
就像，你知道我的意思吗？

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:28.200
Like people are out there like still having conversations with the show a lot.
就好像人们还在外面热火朝天地讨论这部剧。

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:29.600
Yeah, no, it's crazy.
对，没错，这太疯狂了。

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:31.279
It's wild.
太疯狂了。

00:14:31.279 --> 00:14:33.720
I was just on Jesse Tyler Ferguson's podcast.
我刚才上了杰西·泰勒·弗格森的播客。

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:36.759
We were just talking about, you know, because he was on Modern Family for so long.
我们当时在聊，你知道的，因为他在《摩登家庭》里演了那么久。

00:14:36.759 --> 00:14:46.120
And we were talking about like the weirdness of people wanting you to be that character and wanting and interpreting everything you say and do through the lens of the character.
我们聊到人们希望你就是那个角色、通过那个角色的视角来解读你的一言一行，这种现象其实挺诡异的。

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:46.960
So it was strange.
所以这感觉很奇怪。

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:49.519
I mean, I think there was one level it was that.
我的意思是，我觉得一方面是这样。

00:14:49.519 --> 00:14:55.240
And the other level was just, you know, the erosion of anonymity and it was weird.
另一方面就是，匿名的消失，这种感觉真的很怪。

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:56.879
I just found it very destabilizing.
我只是觉得这非常让人心神不宁。

00:14:56.879 --> 00:15:01.000
I'm used to it now, like I know how to walk through an airport and take a few photos of people and move on with my day.
我现在习惯了，比如我知道怎么走过机场，跟人们拍几张照片，然后继续过我的一天。

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:03.679
It's not like, it's not a huge deal.
这其实也算不上什么大事。

00:15:03.679 --> 00:15:07.639
But like at the time, I was really unsettled by it.
但在当时，我真的对此感到很不安。

00:15:07.639 --> 00:15:10.240
Yeah, I would imagine that's true for a lot of actors.
对，我想这对很多演员来说都是如此。

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:13.879
For me, I'm like, I am kind of what I am on TV.
对我而言，我觉得我在电视上差不多就是我本来的样子。

00:15:13.879 --> 00:15:16.240
So it's a different, I'm not playing a character.
所以这不太一样，我不是在演一个角色。

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:18.759
My persona is who I am pretty much.
我的银幕形象基本上就是我自己。

00:15:18.759 --> 00:15:23.879
Luckily, it's even better, I mean, hopefully, it's even better in person, but I don't struggle with that.
幸运的是，甚至更好，我是说，希望在现实中更好吧，但我不会为此感到纠结。

00:15:23.879 --> 00:15:32.600
So I'm always curious to hear actors talk about that because it would be really fucking annoying for people to expect you to be a character in the middle of the day on a street in Los Angeles.
所以我总是很好奇听演员们谈论这个，因为如果在大白天洛杉矶的街头，人们期望你一直是个角色，这真的他妈的太烦人了。

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:33.960
That's really annoying.
这真的很烦人。

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:35.519
Yeah, yeah.
对，对。

00:15:35.519 --> 00:15:45.519
I had a girlfriend years ago who said, man, you're a lot nicer when people come up and say your real name and wanna talk about one of the movies you directed.
多年前我有个女友说过，天哪，当别人走过来叫出你的真名、想聊聊你导演的某部电影时，你态度可好多了。

00:15:45.519 --> 00:15:58.840
You know, like I just had this weird, but I've really, I've made peace so much with it And part of it, the rewatching of the show has brought me some peace where I remember watching these episodes, you know, 20 years ago, and I was so hard on myself.
你知道的，我之前对这事有种奇怪的感觉，但我现在真的已经和解了很多，部分原因是因为重新看这部剧给我带来了一些平静，我记得20年前看这些剧集的时候，我对自己的要求太苛刻了。

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:01.690
But now I watch it and I'm like, you were young.
但现在我再看，就会觉得，你当时还年轻。

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:04.690
You were also doing a really good job in a really hard role.
而且你在一个非常难的角色里也表现得很棒。

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:10.250
Like, I have a lot more compassion for myself at this age, looking back on it.
你看，到了这个年纪，回过头来看，我对当年的自己有了更多同理心。

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:14.289
I also have a lot more understanding of why people love the show in some weird way.
我也更能理解为什么人们会以某种奇怪的方式喜爱这部剧。

00:16:14.289 --> 00:16:17.009
Like, I couldn't understand why people are so obsessed with it.
以前，我简直无法理解为什么人们会对它如此痴迷。

00:16:17.009 --> 00:16:20.470
I mean, I was like, it's good, but why have you dedicated your life to it?
我的意思是，我觉得它挺好，但你为什么要把一生都奉献给它？

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:21.769
Like, why is it a religion?
为什么它就像一种宗教一样？

00:16:21.769 --> 00:16:31.889
But now I get that like, it really teaches people something about those years, like those 25 to 35, which are really pivotal, interesting times in people's lives, you know?
但现在我懂了，因为它确实能让人从那些岁月中有所收获，比如25到35岁这段时期，这真的是人一生中至关重要、充满趣味的阶段，你知道吗？

00:16:31.889 --> 00:16:32.889
Right.
对。

00:16:32.889 --> 00:16:37.570
We're talking about the show, How I Met Your Mother, and then we're talking about Josh's podcast, which is How We Made Your Mother.
我们刚才在聊《老爸老妈浪漫史》这部剧，然后又聊到了乔什的播客，也就是《我们是如何创造你老爸的》。

00:16:37.570 --> 00:16:43.529
It sounds like you've also done a lot of self-reflection and like, probably therapy, I'm assuming, correct?
听起来你也做了很多自我反思，而且我猜可能也做过心理治疗吧？

00:16:43.529 --> 00:16:44.529
Sure, yeah.
当然，是的。

00:16:44.529 --> 00:16:53.889
was reading about you in preparation for this interview, it sounds like you have a lot of like stuff you wanna get out, you know, especially with the music.
我为了准备这次采访而阅读了关于你的报道，听起来你有很多想要倾诉的东西，你知道的，特别是在音乐方面。

00:16:53.889 --> 00:17:02.970
It's nice to have the freedom to be able to direct, to be able to act, to be able to sing like these guitar ballads that are on your album that I was reading some of the lyrics of.
能够拥有导演、演戏、唱歌的自由真好，就像你专辑里的那些吉他民谣，我读过其中的一些歌词。

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:15.250
And it's really just a testament to the creative spirit, I think, when you really can do all of these things aptly and want and have the desire to keep doing it and keep producing things.
我觉得，当你真正能把所有这些事情都做得很好，并且渴望继续做下去、不断创作出作品时，这绝对是对创造精神的最好证明。

00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:16.890
Yeah, yeah, thanks.
对，对，谢谢。

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:22.190
I mean, I, you know, when I was in drama school, like I just wanted to do an episode of Law and Order.
我的意思是，你知道的，我刚进戏剧学校的时候，我只想演一集《法律与秩序》。

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:28.730
And then you do that and you're like, oh, wait, maybe I could actually be on one of these shows for a while or maybe I could be on Broadway.
等你真的演了之后，你就会想，哦，等等，也许我真的可以在这些剧里演上一段时间，或者也许我能去百老汇。

00:17:28.730 --> 00:17:38.970
And the, you know, it's hard because the carrot keeps moving, but at the same time, when you do one thing, it kind of like uncorks this thing of like, oh, what if I tried that or what if I tried that?
你知道的，这很难，因为诱饵一直在往前移，但与此同时，当你做成了一件事，它就会像打开了某个开关一样，让你觉得，哦，要不我试试那个，要不我再试试这个？

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:43.009
And I started writing songs with my friend Den Li, who's a great Australian songwriter.
然后我开始和我的朋友丹·李一起写歌，他是一位出色的澳大利亚词曲作家。

00:17:43.009 --> 00:17:45.210
So I kind of apprenticed with this great songwriter.
所以我有点像是师从这位优秀的词曲作家。

00:17:45.210 --> 00:17:47.049
Like I took it very seriously as a craft.
我把这门手艺当成一门真正的专业来看待。

00:17:47.049 --> 00:17:51.170
You know, I was, I didn't like go in with a producer and be like, here's three lines, make a song.
你知道的，我不是那种去找个制作人然后说“这里有三行词，帮我弄首歌”的人。

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:54.609
Like I really tried to get under the hood of how songs are made.
我是真的试图去深入了解一首歌究竟是怎么创作出来的。

00:17:54.609 --> 00:17:58.859
And it's just been, ah, it's been so satisfying.
这真的是……太让人满足了。

00:17:58.859 --> 00:18:00.740
Oh, that's so nice to hear.
哦，听到你这么说真好。

00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:04.880
Where do you think you find your like, happiest version of yourself?
你觉得你在什么时候能找到最快乐的自己？

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:14.039
I feel like when I have my hooks in a song or like a script and there's like, just like a good writing day is truly the most satisfying thing.
我觉得当我全身心投入到一首歌或是一个剧本里的时候，而且只要有一个不错的写作日，这绝对是最令人满足的事情。

00:18:14.039 --> 00:18:34.440
For me, when you catch the wave, and it's not like, I mean, I don't mind chiseling away at something, but when you catch a wave, there's kind of a thing of the afternoon walk with my dog and then writing a song, and my wife is a psychologist waiting for her to come down from the office and get dinner in our sweet little neighborhood.
对我来说，当你抓住了创作的灵感，当然，我不介意一点点细细打磨一件作品，但当你文思泉涌时，那种感觉就像是：下午带狗狗散步，然后写写歌，我的妻子是一名心理学家，等着她从办公室下来，在我们那个可爱的小社区里一起吃晚饭。

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:41.599
Like pleasures are small these days, and I mean cosmically big, but small in the grand scheme things.
现在的快乐都很小，我是说宇宙级别的大，但从宏观来看其实很小。

00:18:41.599 --> 00:18:47.200
And I've really liked the last, you know, year and a half we've been married has just felt very sweet.
我真的觉得过去这，你知道的，一年半的婚姻生活感觉非常甜蜜。

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:48.200
That's sweet.
真甜蜜。

00:18:48.200 --> 00:18:51.000
I love to hear a straight man talk like this.
我最喜欢听直男这样说话了。

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:53.480
Now, what is your, so your wife's a psychologist?
话说回来，你的，你太太是个心理学家？

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:55.759
How does that play out in your marriage?
这在你们的婚姻中是如何体现的呢？

00:18:55.759 --> 00:19:01.440
It's good because I'm pretty psychologically minded, you know, and I can speak that language.
这挺好的，因为我对心理学挺有共鸣的，你知道的，我懂那种语言。

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:04.799
And she really loves stories and television and movies.
而且她真的很喜欢故事、电视和电影。

00:19:04.799 --> 00:19:11.240
And she has no desire to be in front, but her story instincts are unbelievably good.
她虽然没有抛头露面的欲望，但她对故事的直觉好得难以置信。

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:13.720
like she always knows what's about to happen in something.
比如她总是能提前知道剧情里要发生什么。

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:15.519
She's like always a little bit ahead of things.
她总是能比别人领先一步。

00:19:15.519 --> 00:19:19.579
And when she reads things, I really trust her eye and her ear.
当她阅读时，我真的很信任她的眼光和直觉。

00:19:19.579 --> 00:19:23.720
Like she's a great shadow collaborator in certain ways.
在某些方面，她就像一个出色的幕后合作者。

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:26.960
I just read this term called proleptic irony.
我刚读到过一个词叫“预叙反讽”（proleptic irony）。

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:27.799
Do you know what that is?
你知道那是什么意思吗？

00:19:27.799 --> 00:19:28.640
No.
不知道。

00:19:28.640 --> 00:19:31.039
When the audience knows, it's just what you're describing about your wife.
就是当观众知道，这正符合你刚才对你太太的描述。

00:19:31.039 --> 00:19:36.480
When the audience knows something's gonna happen in a movie or a book before the characters do.
当观众在电影或书中的角色察觉之前，就已经预知了要发生的事情。

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:39.880
So as the reader or as the viewer, it's called proleptic irony.
所以作为读者或观众，这被称为预叙反讽。

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:46.079
And then there's another part of, there's another form of irony, which is not as relevant to this conversation.
然后还有另一种，还有另一种反讽形式，这跟这段对话不太相关。

00:19:46.079 --> 00:19:48.440
But I thought that was such a good thing that there was a term for that.
但我当时觉得能有个词来形容这个真的太棒了。

00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:49.440
I like knowing that.
我很高兴能知道这个。

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:50.440
Yeah.
对。

00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:51.440
Yeah.
对。

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:52.440
Exactly.
没错。

00:19:52.440 --> 00:19:56.519
Does she psychoanalyze you ever, or is she just too tired from working that she doesn't have time for that?
她会 कभी 对你进行心理分析吗？还是说她工作太累了，根本没时间搞这个？

00:19:56.519 --> 00:19:58.480
Legitimately, she is too tired.
说实话，她是真的太累了。

00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:00.799
She doesn't, we don't, we don't really do that.
她不会，我们不会，我们真的不搞那一套。

00:20:00.799 --> 00:20:04.759
I mean, it's more of like, do you want, like, what was that?
我的意思是，更像是，你想要，比如说，那是什么来着？

00:20:04.759 --> 00:20:13.671
I heard a great thing that was like, when, when someone comes to you and they're all worked up, you say, do you want a toolbox, a tissue box, or a soapbox?
我听过一个很棒的说法，就是当，当有人来找你，情绪激动的时候，你会问：你想要工具箱、纸巾箱还是肥皂箱？

00:20:13.671 --> 00:20:16.890
So it's like the soapbox is like, you just want to vent.
肥皂箱的意思就是，你只是想发泄一下情绪。

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:20.283
The toolbox is, do you want some help working through this?
工具箱的意思是，你想找人帮忙解决这个问题吗？

00:20:20.283 --> 00:20:24.480
And the tissue box is like, do you just want to release it and let it go?
而纸巾箱的意思是，你只是想把情绪释放出来，让它过去？

00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:26.233
And I think we're pretty good.
我觉得我们在这方面处理得挺好的。

00:20:26.233 --> 00:20:30.615
I've had enough relationships where I'm like, I don't do the fucking thing.
我经历过足够多的恋爱，以至于我知道自己不会去干那种蠢事。

00:20:30.615 --> 00:20:40.666
I mean, sometimes you fall into it, but I don't get in there and try to fix it, you know, but a lot of times she's so, she's seen, you know, six or seven patients in a day.
我是说，有时候你难免会陷入那种模式，但我不会去强行介入并试图修复它，你知道的，但很多时候她太，她一天要看，你知道的，六七个病人。

00:20:40.666 --> 00:20:42.711
And she's like, and on zoom, right?
而且还是在 Zoom 上，对吧？

00:20:42.711 --> 00:20:45.400
And she is, that is close for business, right?
而且她，那就是打烊关门了，对吧？

00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:49.329
So then we're just figuring out where to go to dinner, you know, yeah, that's nice.
所以接下来我们只需要考虑去哪里吃晚饭，你知道的，对，那挺好的。

00:20:49.329 --> 00:20:52.311
It's nice to figure out where to go to dinner in New York City.
在纽约市考虑去哪里吃晚饭是一件很惬意的事。

00:20:52.311 --> 00:20:52.880
I love that.
我太喜欢了。

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:56.734
We're in Fort Green, which has some of the best restaurants in New York.
我们住在格林堡（Fort Greene），那里有纽约最好的一些餐厅。

00:20:56.734 --> 00:20:58.768
And we went to Italy on our honeymoon.
我们度蜜月的时候去了意大利。

00:20:58.768 --> 00:21:03.640
And we were like, I mean, it's good, but like, it genuinely you to get spoiled in New York.
我们当时觉得，我是说，那里挺好，但说实话，你在纽约真的会被惯坏。

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:04.559
As good as New York.
和纽约一样好。

00:21:04.559 --> 00:21:07.200
I mean, some of it is, yeah, you, sorry, Italy.
我是说，有些地方确实，对吧，对不起了，意大利。

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:08.880
We had a great time, though.
不过我们玩得很开心。

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:10.559
I know, I think you're right, though.
我知道，不过我觉得你是对的。

00:21:10.559 --> 00:21:12.640
Like, it's kind of like Mexico.
这就有点像墨西哥。

00:21:12.640 --> 00:21:18.160
You go to, like, I was in, like, I go, when I was in Mexico City, I was so excited about all the food.
你去，比如，我在，当我身处墨西哥城的时候，我对那里的所有美食兴奋不已。

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:21.079
And I was like, this is not what I envisioned at all.
当时我就觉得，这跟我设想的完全不一样。

00:21:21.079 --> 00:21:24.799
I don't like, I feel like we might have better Mexican food in Los Angeles.
而且我觉得，洛杉矶的墨西哥菜可能还更好吃一点。

00:21:24.799 --> 00:21:27.960
Yeah, it's almost like, do you remember that movie, John Carter?
对，这感觉就像，你还记得那部电影《异星战场》吗？

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:34.240
It was like, it was basically like a novel that George Lucas plundered for all this inspiration for Star Wars.
它基本上就像是一部小说，乔治·卢卡斯从里面抄了好多灵感来拍《星球大战》。

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:38.440
And then they made a John Carter movie and then everyone's like, it's like a Star Wars ripoff.
然后他们拍了部《异星战场》电影，结果每个人都说，这不就是抄袭《星战》嘛。

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:42.519
And it's like, no, that was the original thing that George Lucas loved.
然后事实是，不，那才是乔治·卢卡斯当年深爱的那部原著。

00:21:42.519 --> 00:21:47.839
And I feel like Italian food or Mexican food is probably like that, where it's like, that's the OG thing.
我觉得意大利菜或墨西哥菜大概也是这样，就好像，那是最初的鼻祖。

00:21:47.839 --> 00:21:50.559
But we like took it and made it something else.
但我们拿过来，把它变成了别的东西。

00:21:50.559 --> 00:21:51.559
Yeah, exactly.
对，没错。

00:21:51.559 --> 00:21:53.400
So now it's like, we've acclimated too.
所以现在的情况是，我们已经习惯了。

00:21:53.400 --> 00:21:55.000
And we think that's right.
并且我们觉得这就对了。

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:55.500
Exactly.
没错。

00:21:55.500 --> 00:21:56.000
Exactly.
确实如此。

00:21:55.920 --> 00:21:57.000
Same thing with pizza.
披萨也是一样。

00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:59.000
You know, there's that famous pizzeria in Rome.
你知道，罗马有家很有名的披萨店。

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:00.359
Did you go to Rome on your honeymoon?
你度蜜月的时候去过罗马吗？

